00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.860
[HORROR HISTORY]

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.080
As a young girl, Annie loved going to the
movies with her brother, and her favorite

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:16.850
part was always the chapter plays.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:21.730
In the 1940s, television was not yet widespread,
and the internet did not yet exist, so feature

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:26.000
films were accompanied by newsreels and serials,
or as Annie called them, chapter plays.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.980
Her favorite series was called Rocketman.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:31.010
Well, it may have actually been called King
of the Rocket Men.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:33.220
Annie’s memory tends to be a bit spotty.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.420
She loved the cliffhangers at the end of each
episode, and would spend the week thinking

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.890
about how Rocketman would get out of his latest
dilemma.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:43.020
In one episode, Rocketman was stuck in a car
with no brakes, that went over a cliff, bursted

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.960
into flames and fell into the ocean.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.430
The next week, Annie was there early to see
how it turned out, but was furious to see

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:51.280
Rocketman jump out of the car just before
it went off the cliff.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.280
"I stood right up and started shouting.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.400
This isn't what happened last week.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.260
Have you all got amnesia?

00:01:58.340 --> 00:01:59.980
They just cheated us.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:01.360
This isn't fair!

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.600
He didn’t get out of the cockadoodie car!!”

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.200
She wouldn't stop yelling about how that wasn’t
what happened last week, to the point that

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:13.030
the manager threatens to kick her out.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.050
This incident tells us two things about Annie.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:19.050
First, it highlights her obsessive attitude
towards works of fiction, a problem that will

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:22.880
only intensify as she gets older and discovers
new stories to latch onto.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.980
Second, it shows the early signs of borderline
personality disorder, commonly known as bi

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:27.980
polar.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.060
She’s sitting there excited for the movie
one minute, and causing a scene the next;

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:34.170
she has no control over her emotions and mood
swings.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:38.250
Annie was also a long time sugar-junkie, maybe
another sign of her lack of self discipline.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:42.750
She sometimes babysat for a woman who lived
in her building named Mrs. Kremitz, but she didn’t

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.760
like the four kids, and called them the brats.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:49.850
On October 28th 1954, when Annie was 11, her
family was staying in a temporary house because

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:53.860
of a water leak in the apartment, and Annie
went back and started a fire in the basement,

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:56.870
killing five people, including the brats she
had to babysit.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:01.040
This was an extreme response, showing us that
Annie’s views on other people were very

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.050
black and white.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:05.860
She either liked someone, and practically
worshipped them, or deemed them as bad, and

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.280
felt they didn’t deserve to live.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.920
There was very little in between.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:13.170
Unfortunately for her father Carl Wilkes,
he fell into the latter category.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:17.580
On July 19th, 1957, he passed away at 44 years
of age.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.890
Newspapers reported him stumbling on a pile
of clothing, but it’s heavily implied that

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:23.640
his 14 year old daughter had something to
do with it.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:27.700
"Come here."

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.040
"DAD!"

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:38.820
The next incident came January 29th 1962.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.720
Annie was in her sophomore year at USC’s
nursing school, when her roommate Andrea St.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.430
James was taken out in a similar manner -- she
had supposedly tripped on their cat, which

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:49.550
was lying on the stairs, ending her life at
21 years of age.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:53.290
It was discovered that the cat had also been
poisoned, but that didn’t seem suspicious

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:57.069
because the landlord had put out rat traps
in the basement, and figured the cat had gotten

00:03:57.069 --> 00:03:58.069
into them.

00:03:58.069 --> 00:04:01.750
Perhaps one of the most disturbing parts about
Annie’s minimum of 8 victims before even

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:05.709
graduating from college, is the fact that
there was a sense of pride she associated

00:04:05.709 --> 00:04:06.930
with her accomplishments.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:10.760
She collected newspaper articles for each
of the obituaries she was responsible for,

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:14.810
and secretly kept them in her scrapbook, often
looking back at them as her “cherished memories,”

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:18.849
right there with other normal achievements,
like her graduation from nursing school on

00:04:18.849 --> 00:04:20.789
May 17, 1966.

00:04:20.789 --> 00:04:22.270
She was an honor student.

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:26.319
The first nursing job she held was across
the country in Manchester, NH, where she continued

00:04:26.319 --> 00:04:28.580
to collect obituaries at St. Joseph’s Hospital.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:33.270
Her first victim there was a 79 year old Ernest
Gonyar, followed not long after by Hester

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:34.270
Queeny Builiphant.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:38.279
There were three deaths that summer, then
two more in the fall of 1969.

00:04:38.279 --> 00:04:41.240
So during that year in New Hampshire, she
had nearly doubled her victim count.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.199
I would attribute this to desensitization.

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:47.749
Her attacks in her child and teen years were
very spaced out, but the more she did it,

00:04:47.749 --> 00:04:49.789
the more frequent her issues became.

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:53.370
Then once she was in nursing school, she was
around death a lot more, and got even more

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:54.370
used to it.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:58.060
For most people this wouldn’t be a problem,
but for someone with BPD in addition to a

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:00.499
secret, violent past, it could be considered
an issue.

00:05:00.499 --> 00:05:05.690
I would like to point out that the desensitization
I’m talking about is not the same as desensitization

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:09.759
to violence in movies, video games, or other
media, which has been proven to have no link

00:05:09.759 --> 00:05:11.210
to real world violence.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:15.580
Each of her hospital victims were older people
whose deaths were attributed to “long illness”,

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.300
or in some cases, “short illness.”

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:21.669
Perhaps due to paranoia that people were catching
on, she decided to start fresh in a new town;

00:05:21.669 --> 00:05:23.500
a career reboot, if you will.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:27.970
But like many horror franchises today, Annie’s
life had WAY too many reboots.

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:29.380
Yes, I’m looking at you.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.300
*sad music*

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:38.120
It started on March 19th, 1970, when she was
announced as a new registered nurse at Riverview

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.199
Hospital in Harrisburg Pennsylvania.

00:05:40.199 --> 00:05:43.110
This was followed by another two patients
that she gave the old “you don't get to

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:44.370
be alive anymore” treatment to.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.470
You know Annie is scary when I’m running
out of words to use that won’t get me in

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:48.669
trouble with YouTube.

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:52.499
It was also in Harrisburg where she took up
karate, and apparently was very good at it.

00:05:52.499 --> 00:05:55.900
I would guess that this is also where she
gained a lot of her strength and part of the

00:05:55.900 --> 00:06:00.050
reason she’s able to easily carry a fully
grown man when she’s in her mid 40s.

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.669
Annie also took up a hobby in collecting ceramic
figurines.

00:06:02.669 --> 00:06:06.749
Her last victim in Harrisburg was a 3 year
old who had fallen down a well and was already

00:06:06.749 --> 00:06:11.129
in a coma, so it didn’t look that suspicious
that Annie was "unable to save the kid".

00:06:11.129 --> 00:06:15.189
At this point she basically got into a cycle;
she’d get a job in a new city, collect two

00:06:15.189 --> 00:06:19.639
or three new victims and move on before anyone
started to catch on to what she was up to.

00:06:19.639 --> 00:06:23.770
But the reason she didn’t stay may not have
just been because of her growing list of victims.

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:27.889
She was also secretly stealing medication,
and stashing it for her own personal use.

00:06:27.889 --> 00:06:31.169
“But I knew enough not to take any of the
morphine-based drugs.

00:06:31.169 --> 00:06:32.249
They lock those up.

00:06:32.249 --> 00:06:33.249
They count.

00:06:33.249 --> 00:06:34.249
They keep records.

00:06:34.249 --> 00:06:38.030
And if they get an idea that a nurse is, you
know, chipping—that’s what they call it—they

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:39.990
watch that nurse until they’re sure.”

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:44.229
The non-canon fan series Castle Rock suggests
that she stole medications to relieve her

00:06:44.229 --> 00:06:46.990
hallucinations, but there’s no confirmation
of that in the novel.

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:51.939
Her pattern continued until 1978, when she
started a job at the Denver Receiving Hospital.

00:06:51.939 --> 00:06:56.279
Annie’s first victim in Denver was a woman
named Laura Rothberg, in September of 1978,

00:06:56.279 --> 00:06:59.520
but the next headline would not be an obituary,
but rather…

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.500
a marriage announcement?

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:16.430
Ralph Dugan was a physical therapist at Arapahoe
County Hospital, and according to Paul Sheldon,

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:18.509
he looked a lot like Annie Wilkes’s father.

00:07:18.509 --> 00:07:21.990
Marrying a parent like figure has come up
before in King’s writing, I discussed similar

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:26.020
relationships when analyzing characters like
Beverly Marsh and Eddie Kaspbrak.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:29.931
Annie relocated an hour Northwest to the small
town of Nederland where Ralph’s hospital

00:07:29.931 --> 00:07:30.931
was located.

00:07:30.931 --> 00:07:35.449
Together, they bought a house in Sidewinder
on March 3rd, 1979 and during the time she

00:07:35.449 --> 00:07:38.699
was with Ralph, she didn’t add any obituaries
to her collection.

00:07:38.699 --> 00:07:42.139
Someone with Borderline Personality Disorder
may try to use a relationship as a source

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:45.689
of comfort from their symptoms, the same way
that struggling people might turn to drugs

00:07:45.689 --> 00:07:49.869
or alcohol, so I think this might have contributed
to her break period from the violence.

00:07:49.869 --> 00:07:54.189
Unfortunately, Ralph was a physical therapist,
not a mental one, so leaning on a relationship

00:07:54.189 --> 00:07:56.800
was probably not a healthy way to deal with
her issue.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:00.909
Kimberly Holland writes on Healthline, that
even common BPD symptoms can be detrimental

00:08:00.909 --> 00:08:04.199
to a relationship, and that these endeavors
are often short lived.

00:08:04.199 --> 00:08:08.939
Ralph and Annie lasted only a year and a half,
and on August 3rd, 1980, Ralph filed for divorce

00:08:08.939 --> 00:08:10.699
on the grounds of mental cruelty.

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:14.879
Paul suggests that Ralph might have realized
her true nature, and luckily gotten out of

00:08:14.879 --> 00:08:16.400
there while he still had his life.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:20.369
Another note on the BPD in relationships article
states that “(the person) can become angry

00:08:20.369 --> 00:08:23.860
and hurt over something a person without BPD
would not react to.

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:25.529
They can even become obsessive.”

00:08:25.529 --> 00:08:29.509
However, as far as we can tell, Annie does
not necessarily become obsessive about her

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:33.069
ex-husband, but rather, she focuses on her
other true love: fiction.

00:08:33.069 --> 00:08:35.690
More specifically, she’s found a new favorite
author.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:39.611
His name was Paul Sheldon, and Annie adored
his period romance novel series, the Misery

00:08:39.611 --> 00:08:40.611
books.

00:08:40.611 --> 00:08:41.979
Misery had become her new obsession.

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:45.860
Annie also loved pigs and even named her newest
addition to the barn after Misery’s titular

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:46.680
character.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:51.360
"Paul, say hello to my favorite beast in the whole world.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:53.040
My sow, Misery!"

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:54.800
"Oink,  oink, oink."

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:55.540
"Misery."

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:59.360
..and I think after finding out that the author
traditionally finished his novels nearby in

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:04.180
Boulder, Colorado, the object of her obsession
turned from Misery to him and she relocated

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:06.780
her job one last time to a hospital in Boulder.

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:11.009
By the time she started that job in March
1981, she had taken back her maiden name;

00:09:11.009 --> 00:09:13.410
Annie Dugan was once again Annie Wilkes.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:17.160
It would also seem that her anger over the
failed marriage was taken out on her patients

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:21.990
in Boulder, and in 1981, she made up for her
lost time by taking 13 more victims from May

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:25.120
until the end of the year, bringing her total
up over 30.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:27.980
But the most disgusting of her crimes were
yet to come.

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:32.769
On January 14th, 1982 she was named as the
new head maternity nurse, and it only took

00:09:32.769 --> 00:09:36.899
two weeks to extend her tradition onto the
newborn infants that were kept there.

00:09:36.899 --> 00:09:41.330
The first incident took place January 29th,
with another that followed not long after.

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:45.060
When Paul eventually reads about the newborn
obituaries in Annie’s scrapbook, he thinks

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:49.069
she probably started only going after babies
with birth defects, and eventually expanded

00:09:49.069 --> 00:09:51.009
it to include healthy ones as well.

00:09:51.009 --> 00:09:54.620
This theory makes sense, because she at one
point demonstrates to Paul that she believes

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:58.210
in putting creatures out of their misery when
she kills an injured rat, but she laters shows

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:02.560
evidence of a growing taste for violence when
she re-injures Paul after noticing he’s

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:03.560
starting to heal.

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:08.140
In mid-March there are five more nursery deaths,
and this time an investigation is launched.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:12.640
"We are going to be launching an investigation into what happened.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:15.640
I told them that we were launching an investigation.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:17.720
*laughter*

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:21.460
Previously she was always able to get away
before her actions were investigated, but

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:24.680
this time she wasn’t able to do so, and
I can see two reasons.

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:28.360
First, the fact that she was dealing with
infants this time as opposed to elderly people.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:33.339
It’s not THAT unusual for an elderly person
to get sick and pass away, and while newborns

00:10:33.339 --> 00:10:37.760
are lost from time to time, it doesn’t make
sense for so many healthy babies to kick the bucket.

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:40.420
The second reason goes back to her short lived
marriage with Ralph.

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:44.370
It’s much easier to pick up and leave unexpectedly
if you’re renting an apartment, but since

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:48.420
she owned the house now, it would be much
harder to move, not to mention, more suspicious.

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:52.480
But the amazing part about this junction in
her history, is that the investigation didn’t

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:53.680
discourage her from continuing.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:57.870
On March 24th, she made a statement on behalf
of the hospital, saying that the culprit may

00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:02.639
have been tainted baby formula, but in April,
another infant hit zero HP and in May two

00:11:02.639 --> 00:11:05.529
more little chonkers took dirt naps instead
of nursery naps.

00:11:05.529 --> 00:11:09.899
By June 1st, they were closing in on Annie,
and she underwent questioning by her own free

00:11:09.899 --> 00:11:10.899
will.

00:11:10.899 --> 00:11:14.589
They eventually let her go, and maybe that
close call got her to back off temporarily,

00:11:14.589 --> 00:11:18.759
because there were no infant cases in June,
but she started back up again in early July

00:11:18.759 --> 00:11:20.889
with three more little tiny funerals.

00:11:20.889 --> 00:11:25.160
On July 19th, Annie was arrested for the murder
of Girl Christopher, one of the victims that

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:27.149
actually lived long enough to be given a name.

00:11:27.149 --> 00:11:30.579
Annie was given a nickname by the press, The
Dragon Lady, because of the nature of the

00:11:30.579 --> 00:11:34.649
situation where the entire village was basically
after her with torches and pitchforks.

00:11:34.649 --> 00:11:38.410
The best evidence against her was a handmark
on the child’s throat with what seemed to

00:11:38.410 --> 00:11:39.879
be the mark of Annie’s ring.

00:11:39.879 --> 00:11:44.129
The jury made their verdict on December 13th,
and in the newspaper was a photograph of Annie

00:11:44.129 --> 00:11:47.699
quietly reading her copy of Misery’s Quest
inside her holding cell.

00:11:47.699 --> 00:11:51.209
The Dragon Lady was found innocent, much to
the dismay of the town.

00:11:51.209 --> 00:11:54.630
She parted ways with the hospital, putting
an end to what would be her last job, but

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:56.959
that did not mean that she’d put out her
last victim.

00:11:56.959 --> 00:12:01.740
In late 1983 she picked up a hitchhiker named
Andrew Pomeroy on her way back from a ceramics

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:02.320
show.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:06.260
Pomeroy told her he was headed to Sidewinder
on assignment from a magazine in New York;

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:09.750
he was to go make some sketches of the ruins
of a place called the Overlook Hotel, which

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:12.990
had been burned down 10 years beforehand by
a crazy caretaker.

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:16.050
Those who have read The Shining will be familiar
with this location.

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:20.199
There are often cross-references to other
stories in King’s writing, but this connection

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:21.580
could share a deeper meaning.

00:12:21.580 --> 00:12:25.040
Misery is almost like a sister or companion
piece to The Shining.

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:28.720
Both stories feature authors working on a
new project who suffer from the psychological

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:32.079
effects of isolation, while stranded in Sidewinder,
Colorado.

00:12:32.079 --> 00:12:35.350
The big difference is that The Shining is
a ghost story, while Misery has no direct

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:39.259
references to anything supernatural at all,
however there are many scenes where Paul and

00:12:39.259 --> 00:12:42.600
Annie try to get into each other’s head
and figure out what the other one is thinking,

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:46.420
though it's not evident that either one is
successful in the mind reading tactic know

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:47.420
as 'shining'.

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:51.240
There’s also something interesting about
the weapon of choice in each respective story.

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:55.310
In the book, Annie uses and axe, but in the
movie she uses a mallet -- whereas in The

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:58.579
Shining it’s a mallet in the book, but an
axe in the movie.

00:12:58.579 --> 00:13:02.310
Pomeroy ends up staying at Annie’s house,
where, according to her, they were lovers,

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:04.690
despite him being 18 years younger at the
age of 22.

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:08.710
However, it was bad news for him when Annie
found out he’d been lying about working

00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:12.079
for the magazine, he was really just planning
to sell the drawings on spec.

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:14.720
She was also critical of his work, which caused
him to laugh at her,

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:16.020
"They're all gonna laugh at you!"

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:17.180
and that may have been

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:21.470
the last straw because Annie gave him the
axe and dumped his body in the frozen streambed,

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:23.160
where it stayed for the entire winter.

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:26.850
Once the stream thawed out, the water carried
his dismembered remains away -- nearly 27

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:31.689
miles -- and he wasn’t found until November
1984, a year after his death.

00:13:31.689 --> 00:13:36.350
Annie continued living in relative isolation
with her 6 hens, 2 cows and her pig, Misery.

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:39.790
She did sometimes get complaints about her
yard from her neighbors, The Roidmans, but

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:43.970
it would seem that with no hospital to feed
her new victims, her homicidal days were behind

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:48.720
her, but in February 1987, she would discover
a golden opportunity.

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:58.560
On a stormy day in mid-to-late February, Annie
went into town to get groceries and check

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:02.189
if the paperbacks of the new Paul Sheldon
book, Misery’s Child, were in yet.

00:14:02.189 --> 00:14:05.459
While her copy of the book was not ready yet,
she would get something much better.

00:14:05.459 --> 00:14:07.480
And I’m not talking about the audiobook.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:11.240
She ended up coming across a car crash, and
because of her nursing background, she can

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:14.550
tell that the victim has the will to live
based on the quality of his screams.

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:18.449
Now in the movie she eventually admits that
she was stalking Paul when the accident occurred;

00:14:18.449 --> 00:14:21.620
that’s not the case in the book, but I do
like to think that she knew of his tradition

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:26.029
of finishing his books at the Boulderado Hotel
and maybe went out of her way to go past there.

00:14:26.029 --> 00:14:30.220
The CPR she administered probably saved his
life, but because of her obsessive fandom,

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:34.220
she makes the decision not to do the right
thing by bringing him to the hospital, but

00:14:34.220 --> 00:14:37.629
instead, back to her house, where she planned
to take care of him on her own.

00:14:37.629 --> 00:14:40.259
Annie already considered herself his number
one fan.

00:14:40.259 --> 00:14:43.230
She had taken a liking to his work immediately
when she discovered it.

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:47.180
But because this is where the story starts,
this is where we can shift our focus from

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:51.060
Annie’s Borderline Personality Disorder
to another classification, Celebrity Worship

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:51.820
Syndrome.

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:53.759
I’m not a fan of that acronym.

00:14:53.759 --> 00:14:57.930
But unlike BPD, Celebrity Worship Syndrome
isn’t officially recognized by the National

00:14:57.930 --> 00:15:01.620
Institute of Mental Health, but remember these
disorders are just ways for us to describe

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:06.139
a pattern of mental behavior, it’s not quite
the same as say, a virus which has physical

00:15:06.139 --> 00:15:08.050
particles that we can identify.

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:12.040
But there is still legit research on Celebrity
Worship Syndrome, with studies linking it

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:14.660
to increased levels of anxiety and depression.

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:18.430
Both of those are also symptoms of Borderline
Personality Disorder, which obviously led

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:22.310
me to speculate that in Annie's case the two
are linked, and I’ll discuss that link later

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:23.430
on in her story.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:27.800
Mr. Sheldon falls into a respiratory depression,
so Annie had him on pain medication and fed

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:28.800
him through an IV.

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:32.320
A week and a half later she went into town
where she was finally able to get her copy

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:33.300
of Misery's Child.

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:40.320
"Paul Sheldon writes the Misery novels. They're very good. I'm looking for the latest one.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:45.420
Misery's Child. I really need that to complete my collection.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:48.080
And I really think you need to have that here!

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:48.800
Misery!"

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:51.200
"I don't have it in the store but I could probably order it for you."

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:53.080
"I'm not interested in waiting.

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:56.040
What kind of cockadoodie bookstore is this?

00:15:56.200 --> 00:16:02.260
How can ANY of you BE in a BOOKSTORE when you don't know PAUL SHELDON?

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:09.300
"I AM LOOKING FOR A COCKADOODIE BOOOOOKKKK!!!!!!!!!"

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:09.860
"...I can order it for you."

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:13.400
...and 2 weeks after the crash she greeted
him as he finally came to.

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:17.550
As part of her plan, she kept him on a pain
medication Noveryl, which worked to her advantage

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:20.529
because it made him drowsy so that he was
easy to control.

00:16:20.529 --> 00:16:24.199
She knowingly gets him hooked so that even
as he regained his strength, he would always

00:16:24.199 --> 00:16:26.720
need to stick around to get his fix of Noveryl.

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:30.730
She also finds the manuscript for his next
novel, Fast Cars, in his bag and asks if she

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:31.730
can read it.

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:35.020
When he allows her, she accidentally lets
this confession slip.

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:37.900
“I love you Paul.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:41.740
I mean, your mind.

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:43.280
Your creativity.

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:45.100
That's all I meant.”

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:50.040
This is another possible symptom of the Celebrity Worship
Disorder, commonly referred to as Love Obsessional,

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:53.689
where the person may convince themselves that
they are in love with the celebrity despite

00:16:53.689 --> 00:16:55.339
not actually knowing them.

00:16:55.339 --> 00:16:59.100
This famously happened when a woman convinced
herself she was the wife of talk show host

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:03.399
David Letterman for five years, or perhaps
even more cringefully, a man with a YouTube

00:17:03.399 --> 00:17:07.820
account who publicly released a happy birthday
video for Bridgette West from the Angry Grandpa

00:17:07.820 --> 00:17:11.270
Show where he basically asks her to leave
her boyfriend for him.

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:12.020
Gotta roll it.

00:17:15.340 --> 00:17:16.540
"Bridgette West.

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:20.300
If you liked what you saw, please subscribe to my YouTube channel.

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:24.600
Also, in case you're wondering, the original inspiration to make it look like I'm interested in you,

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:28.140
was a vlog back in June called Pickle Boy and Bridgette Break Up.

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:32.260
But I understand that it will humiliated Michael if you really do break up with him.

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:33.760
But it's your choice.

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:34.920
EEEE….

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:37.640
Giving Annie Wilkes a run for her money there
my man.

00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:41.360
Annie reads 40 pages of the manuscript for
Fast Cars, and criticizes it.

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:45.570
In particular, she’s not a fan of the use
of profanity, and it causes her to smash Paul’s

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:46.670
soup bowl against the wall.

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:50.300
I honestly think this moment is way ahead
of its time, because it demonstrates a rather

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:53.640
new symptom of her disorder, which I’ll
just call toxic fandom.

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:57.140
Annie is upset that Paul is straying from
writing the type of novel that she wants him

00:17:57.140 --> 00:17:57.980
to write.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:18:00.940
Just in the last couple years, we’ve seen
this happen with several franchises.

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:05.450
Fans start to think that they know better than
the artists, they demand a “return to form”

00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:09.490
and then the artist either decides to stay the
course, or cave into the fans’ demands.

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:13.490
Once in a blue moon, the fan imposed changes actually
end up working out for the better, but it’s

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:18.150
really not worth sacrificing all the original
ideas that have been, and probably will continue

00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:19.960
to be thrown out thanks to this trend.

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:24.010
I’m not saying fan feedback is always a
bad thing, but when suggestions turn into

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:28.420
demands, threats, and thinking they know best,
that’s where it crosses over into disease

00:18:28.420 --> 00:18:29.100
territory.

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:33.260
And I’d rather have an artist try something
new and fail than pander to the masses, which

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:35.440
almost never yields an interesting product.

00:18:35.440 --> 00:18:39.170
Modern fans use social media to wage warfare
on the artists they follow, but Annie’s

00:18:39.170 --> 00:18:43.260
method of manipulation involved withholding
his pain medication all afternoon and eventually

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:45.470
making him wash the pills down with rinse
water.

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:49.340
Then, the next morning, she repeats her behaviour
after reading about her favorite character’s

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:50.920
death at the end of Misery’s Child.

00:18:50.920 --> 00:18:54.800
She throws another tantrum, where she destroys
a water pitcher and decides to leave to get

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:56.450
a hold of herself away from Paul.

00:18:56.450 --> 00:18:59.880
She goes to a property she refers to as her
“laughing place”, an upcountry getaway

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:03.560
inspired by the old childrens’ stories of
Brer Rabbit and Brer Fox.

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:07.230
At one point, she explains, “Sometimes I
do laugh when I go there.

00:19:07.230 --> 00:19:08.790
But mostly I just scream.”

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:12.500
While she was there, she prayed to God, and
when she left the laughing place two days

00:19:12.500 --> 00:19:16.340
later, she had the idea that she was supposed
to show Paul the error of his past by making

00:19:16.340 --> 00:19:17.750
him burn his manuscript.

00:19:17.750 --> 00:19:21.150
She also gets the idea to give him the chance
to redeem himself by forcing him to write

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:25.890
a new book, so on the way back she stops in
town and purchases a typewriter and some paper.

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:29.780
She bargains with the shop owner, Nancy Dartmonger,
about the price, because the typewriter is

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:31.020
missing the letter N.

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:34.660
She brings her charcoal grill into the room
and withholds Paul’s food and medication

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:38.150
until he burns Fast Cars, which he does after
putting up a struggle.

00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:41.920
That afternoon she sets up a workstation and
tells him to write a new novel just for her

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:45.410
called Misery’s Return, which was to be
her payment for nursing him.

00:19:45.410 --> 00:19:49.710
Of course, Paul doesn’t have any intention
of writing another Misery novel, but he doesn't really

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:53.140
have a choice -- he needs Annie’s
medical help if he wants to survive, and the

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:57.320
situation is representative of what any artist
has to go through in dealing with these crazy

00:19:57.320 --> 00:19:58.320
fans.

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:01.290
For most artists, they have to balance what
they want to do and what the fans want them

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:05.040
to do to survive… that is, to make money
through their craft while still expressing

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:05.780
themselves.

00:20:05.780 --> 00:20:09.060
In Paul’s case, the survival aspect is much
more literal.

00:20:09.070 --> 00:20:12.620
Annie also tries to manipulate him into going
with her plan by lying and saying he should

00:20:12.620 --> 00:20:15.410
be healthy enough to go out and meet people
again by the time he’s done.

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:19.250
What she’s really doing is sending a subtle
threat, a “do what I say if you want any

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:20.570
chance at surviving.”

00:20:20.570 --> 00:20:23.950
The next morning she shakes Paul awake to
serve his breakfast, excited for him to start

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:25.020
her masterpiece.

00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:28.430
Paul asks that she give him a different type
of paper to work on, one that doesn’t smudge.

00:20:28.430 --> 00:20:31.900
He tries to give her the idea that there are
a lot more complications in the writing business

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:35.670
than she knows about, and I find it interesting
that Annie doesn’t like hearing Paul call

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:36.670
it a business.

00:20:36.670 --> 00:20:40.270
In the same way she can’t see that people
have both good and bad qualities, she also

00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:44.340
refuses to acknowledge that her favorite novels
are written for both art and profit.

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:48.940
And as you’re about to find out, the same concept
also applies to YouTube videos.

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:03.800
After throwing a fit over his request, but
returning from town with the new packs of

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:07.400
paper, Annie returns to find Paul in a sweating,
shivering state.

00:21:07.400 --> 00:21:10.570
This gives her the earliest suspicion that
he’d been trying to escape, but she’s

00:21:10.570 --> 00:21:13.910
able to dispel the thought when he tells her
he’s just been suffering because of the

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:15.390
lack of pain medication.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:17.930
The occurrence would, however, trigger her
paranoia.

00:21:17.930 --> 00:21:21.630
She had initially begun experiencing paranoia
when she felt the whole town was out to get

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:25.830
her during the Dragon Lady trial, so she would
put herself on high alert, which was dangerous

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:26.520
for Paul.

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:30.260
Her suspicions are heightened again when she
notices that one of her ceramic figures in

00:21:30.260 --> 00:21:31.580
the parlor had been moved.

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:35.490
And she wonders if Paul had somehow been out of
the room, but again, begins to second guess

00:21:35.490 --> 00:21:38.440
herself, thinking that she may have been the one to do it herself.

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:42.030
She double checks to make sure she still had
the only key to his door, and she did, but

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:45.630
she still thought that he maybe had found
another way out, and her years working as

00:21:45.630 --> 00:21:48.280
a nurse had taught her to always check her
maybes.

00:21:48.280 --> 00:21:51.870
So she looks in the downstairs bathroom, where
all the medication she’d chipped from her

00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:53.120
various hospital jobs was kept.

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:56.590
Again, she had the sense that stuff had been
stirred around, but couldn’t be sure that

00:21:56.590 --> 00:22:00.360
she hadn’t moved anything herself, so she
decides not to say anything yet, but keep

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:02.190
one eye open for more activity.

00:22:02.190 --> 00:22:05.700
The sequence demonstrates that she may be
crazy, but she still has her wits about

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:08.350
her, and could be even more dangerous because
of her cleverness.

00:22:08.350 --> 00:22:12.350
But two days later, she had been unable to
find more evidence that Paul had left the

00:22:12.350 --> 00:22:16.820
room, so she was about to drop the idea until
she came in to give him his Noveryl one afternoon

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:18.670
and felt the doorknob get stuck.

00:22:18.670 --> 00:22:22.080
When she opened it, she heard something rattle
inside the lock -- but again, she shows her

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:25.100
cleverness by not overreacting until she was
sure.

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:29.120
This is because if he was sneaking around,
she didn’t want him to know she was on to him.

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:32.860
She was better off keeping it a secret so
that Paul would continue to be careless and

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:37.030
possibly leave more evidence behind, thus
making it less likely he'd be able to one

00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:38.030
day sneak away.

00:22:38.030 --> 00:22:40.340
It was a constant mind game between the two
of them.

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:43.790
So when she heard the lock rattle, she gave
him his pills like normal, and acted like

00:22:43.790 --> 00:22:44.790
nothing was wrong.

00:22:44.790 --> 00:22:48.610
But again, she was back on high alert, so
that afternoon, when she helped him into his

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:51.940
wheelchair she realized for the first time
that he was more lively.

00:22:51.940 --> 00:22:54.990
He was able to move his legs more and his
arms were stronger.

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:59.090
She had discovered that he had been hiding
his recovery, and thought that he too was

00:22:59.090 --> 00:23:00.140
good at keeping secrets.

00:23:00.140 --> 00:23:04.070
So that night she countered with a move of
her own by giving him a stronger medication

00:23:04.070 --> 00:23:07.830
than usual to keep him asleep as she removed
the keyplate from the door and discovered

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:09.810
the bobby pin that he’d used as his key.

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:13.300
That’s when she also noticed black marks
on the doorframe, as if he’d bumped the

00:23:13.300 --> 00:23:15.450
wheelchair into it while trying to get through.

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:19.140
Again, she decides it’s too early to confront
him and it’s better if he thinks she’s

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:19.640
oblivious.

00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:23.180
A couple days later, now past the three-week
mark of her kidnapping him, he’s written

00:23:23.190 --> 00:23:27.480
the first chapter of Misery’s Return, but
Annie rejects it for the same reason she detested

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:31.360
the cliff car resolution in Rocketman all
those years ago, because the chapter he came

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:33.960
up with wasn’t consistent with the previous
entry.

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:37.650
As Paul got back to work on a second attempt,
Annie secretly watched him, hoping to gather

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:39.870
more information about his escape plan.

00:23:39.870 --> 00:23:43.350
On March 25th, six chapters are complete,
and Annie agrees that they’re fair.

00:23:43.350 --> 00:23:47.650
She agrees to read his work chapter by chapter
so she can fill in the Ns that he was unable

00:23:47.650 --> 00:23:51.490
to produce with the defective typewriter,
or at least that’s the reason she gives.

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:54.280
The battle between Paul and Annie is a mental
game of chess.

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:57.901
If Annie’s move was to get Paul addicted
to the Noveryl so that he needed her, his

00:23:57.901 --> 00:24:02.160
counter-move was to get her addicted to his
writing so she needed him just as much.

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:05.870
It was also on this day that she gave the
only suggestion about the story she would

00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:09.520
ever give, giving him the idea that maybe
one of the characters had not died, but had

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:12.300
actually fallen into a coma induced by a bee-sting.

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:16.490
Annie seems very embarrassed to give any input,
which is a strange touch because of the control

00:24:16.490 --> 00:24:17.930
she has over the situation.

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:21.210
It shows Paul that she’s still very sensitive
when it comes to the book, and allows him

00:24:21.210 --> 00:24:23.840
to take one of the few advantages that he
can over her.

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:26.200
But this small win for Paul would be short
lived.

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:29.220
Annie hears a car pulling up to her house,
and quickly goes to get her handcuffs and

00:24:29.220 --> 00:24:31.940
the only thing she can find to silence him,
a dust rag.

00:24:31.940 --> 00:24:35.720
She locks her prisoner in place and stuffs
the rag in his mouth, not bothering to take

00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:38.550
the taste of the chemicals left over on it
into consideration.

00:24:38.550 --> 00:24:41.900
Even though she felt that she had prevented
Paul from getting the man’s attention, she

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:46.420
also had to prevent the man from noticing
Paul, since the room does have a window.

00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:49.799
So she walks him up the driveway to angle
off his possible line of sight.

00:24:49.799 --> 00:24:53.980
The guy gives her a document that tells her
that she has an overdue property tax payment,

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:57.480
and she explodes on him with anger, even though
the guy was actually doing her a courtesy

00:24:57.480 --> 00:24:58.570
by notifying her.

00:24:58.570 --> 00:25:02.800
She’s still fuming when she comes back inside,
and Paul helps calm her down by explaining

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:05.710
it to her and offering to pay the fee with
the money left inside of his wallet.

00:25:05.710 --> 00:25:09.470
Annie had basically just forgotten to pay
this fee, one of many examples of her losing

00:25:09.470 --> 00:25:13.400
track of small tasks, like how she forgets
to turn the calendar page in Paul’s room,

00:25:13.400 --> 00:25:16.860
and this isn’t a symptom of the two disorders
we’ve already discussed, but it does show

00:25:16.860 --> 00:25:20.460
us that she’s slowly experiencing an overall
mental degradation.

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:24.090
With Paul’s help, she’s able to go into
town that day and take care of the tax.

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:28.260
From March 25th to April 15th, they have routine
going, where Paul would work on the novel

00:25:28.260 --> 00:25:31.860
and Annie would mostly work on maintaining
her yard in order to give nobody a reason

00:25:31.860 --> 00:25:32.860
to bother her.

00:25:32.860 --> 00:25:36.890
She also cooked and cleaned, she would bring
him dinner at 5PM and afterwards roll in a

00:25:36.890 --> 00:25:40.500
black and white television so they would watch
MASH or WKRP.

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:42.140
Maybe even some Mukbang videos

00:25:52.700 --> 00:25:53.780
Nah.

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:55.220
Not even Annie Wilkes would watch that.

00:25:55.220 --> 00:25:57.210
When
it got warmer she would take him out onto

00:25:57.210 --> 00:25:58.920
the back porch for some fresh air.

00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:03.540
But on April 15th, her good spirits came crashing
back down, and despite the date, this one

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:04.890
was not tax related.

00:26:04.890 --> 00:26:09.620
The main symptom associated with BPD is huge
mood swings, and when this happens, victims

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:12.760
are more vulnerable for self-harm and self-destruction.

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:16.230
On this day, Annie’s mood was swinging like
a 2015 Javier Baez.

00:26:16.230 --> 00:26:19.790
We don’t know exactly how she got into such
a bad state but she’s described as having

00:26:19.790 --> 00:26:23.640
a bloody lip, looking like a mess and having
some kind of goo spilled all over her.

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:27.640
From what I can gather, maybe she was making
breakfast, spilled on herself, got really

00:26:27.640 --> 00:26:30.090
depressed and literally beat herself up over
it.

00:26:30.090 --> 00:26:34.110
She threw Paul his pills, and when asked if
she was OK, she responded, “no”.

00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:37.730
She left him to go sit down in the parlor,
where she repeatedly slapped herself loud

00:26:37.730 --> 00:26:39.550
enough for Paul to hear from his room.

00:26:39.550 --> 00:26:43.250
For a manic-depressive person like Annie,
this was the first sign she was about to slip

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:47.780
into a deep period of depression, which not
only included hurting herself, but also unhealthy

00:26:47.780 --> 00:26:51.800
binge eating that mirrors her lack of self
control when it comes to the consumption of

00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:53.330
fiction, which comes up again later.

00:26:53.330 --> 00:26:56.950
I think this also brought back some of her
paranoia about Paul escaping, and the idea

00:26:56.950 --> 00:27:00.380
of losing the man she thought she loved made
her even more depressed.

00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:03.700
So she ended up spying on him all morning
through the keyhole in the door, which is

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:08.130
representative of another toxic practice seen
in those with Celebrity Worship Syndrome -- something

00:27:08.130 --> 00:27:12.320
that we’ve seen quite a lot in recent years
with YouTube celebrities: invasion of privacy.

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:16.060
There has long been an issue for celebrities
-- where paparazzi would try to capture every

00:27:16.060 --> 00:27:19.590
detail of their lives, and while I don’t
think invading the private portion of someone’s

00:27:19.590 --> 00:27:23.240
life is ever a good thing, we’ve seen it
change from celebrity obsessed individuals

00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:27.590
simply being the market for tablet magazines,
to people tracking down celebrities’ home

00:27:27.590 --> 00:27:31.960
addresses and showing up themselves, or worse,
setting a horrible example for their kids

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:35.240
by bringing them to ambush their favorite
creators at home.

00:27:35.240 --> 00:27:39.450
Back in the day with traditional media celebrities
it was an issue, but less of an issue, because

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:42.150
they could just hire private security and
they were protected.

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:46.240
But now with YouTube and TikTok, we have a
lot of very young people who become very famous

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:49.010
without the wealthiness that used to come
with it.

00:27:49.010 --> 00:27:51.710
So… you know… the merch store is always...
open…

00:27:51.710 --> 00:27:54.720
The reason I bring that up with Paul is he
has to deal with something similar.

00:27:54.720 --> 00:27:58.640
He is rich, but because he’s at the mercy
of his captor, he has no way of protecting

00:27:58.640 --> 00:28:02.590
his privacy against Annie, and she sees him
through the keyhole, getting into his wheelchair

00:28:02.590 --> 00:28:07.040
to write and confirming her suspicions about
him faking how weak he really was.

00:28:07.040 --> 00:28:10.920
This would lead to another outburst where
Annie’s willingness to let him live comes

00:28:10.920 --> 00:28:11.760
to an end.

00:28:18.980 --> 00:28:23.260
Before confronting her disobedient prisoner
again, Annie goes down into the basement to

00:28:23.260 --> 00:28:26.560
clear out the rat traps, because it had been
rainy, and the rats tend to come into the

00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:27.700
cellar when it rains.

00:28:27.700 --> 00:28:31.800
She finds one that is injured, but still alive
and brings it up when she goes to see Paul.

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:35.820
The reason she does this is to draw the comparison
between Paul’s situation and the rat’s

00:28:35.820 --> 00:28:40.070
-- they are both captives of Annie, and because
of their injuries, they’re unable to escape.

00:28:40.070 --> 00:28:42.900
She explains that a rat in a trap thinks it
wants to live.

00:28:42.900 --> 00:28:44.560
“As we do, Paul.

00:28:44.560 --> 00:28:45.560
As we do.

00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:49.451
We think we know so much, but we really don’t
know any more than a rat in a trap—a rat

00:28:49.460 --> 00:28:52.120
with a broken back that thinks it still wants
to live.”

00:28:52.120 --> 00:28:56.240
Notice the wording -- she’s including herself
in that too, because she’s aware of her

00:28:56.240 --> 00:28:59.120
severe depression that is making her miserable
as well.

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:03.650
She crushes the rat in her hand, and likewise,
offers to get her gun and do the same to Paul

00:29:03.650 --> 00:29:04.340
and then herself.

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:08.260
Paul’s only saving grace is to say he first
wants to finish the book, and she agrees that’s

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:11.590
the only thing she still wants to see, and
she mindlessly licks the rat’s blood off

00:29:11.590 --> 00:29:15.280
her fingers as she spoke, which is pretty
disgusting, and I think it’s there to show

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:19.240
us how comfortable Annie is with carnage and
make her threat feel that much more real and

00:29:19.300 --> 00:29:19.980
scary.

00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:23.500
Before leaving, she wants to keep track of
if Paul is moving around the house while she’s

00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:24.120
gone.

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:28.020
She plucks out several hairs from her head
and tapes them up around the house, on doors,

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:31.700
on her scrap book that documents all of her
victims, cabinets, drawers and more.

00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:35.180
This way she could check which threads were
broken when she got back and theoretically

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:38.360
see exactly where Paul had been and what he
had gone through.

00:29:38.360 --> 00:29:42.380
She goes off to her Laughing Place again,
and again, does more praying than laughing.

00:29:42.380 --> 00:29:45.880
Because of the snow she has to put chains
on her tires to get back down the road.

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:49.450
She leaves to come back on April 17th, even
though the conditions were very dangerous,

00:29:49.450 --> 00:29:52.290
but she was not afraid, thinking God would
guide her.

00:29:52.290 --> 00:29:54.900
I’m guessing that she has not seen Carrie.

00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:58.520
She almost slid off the road twice, at one
point going all Time Traveler at Silver Dollar

00:29:58.520 --> 00:30:02.640
City and doing a full 360° spin while traveling
down the slope.

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:06.400
Then around midnight, her car got stuck in
a snowbank, but she was lucky enough to get

00:30:06.410 --> 00:30:09.160
a hand from a public works crew that was able
to free her.

00:30:09.160 --> 00:30:13.140
So she probably was quite confident that God
actually was guiding her on this trip.

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:16.970
She stopped off on Route 9 to see if she could
see Paul’s crashed car, but it was gone,

00:30:16.970 --> 00:30:20.210
which was a relief for her because she wouldn’t
have to worry about anyone finding it and

00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:22.290
coming to look for him for a while longer.

00:30:22.290 --> 00:30:25.970
She finally made it back at 4AM, and finds
her victim fast asleep.

00:30:25.970 --> 00:30:29.340
She comes back and inspects everywhere she
had set up her hairs, finding many of the

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:32.280
threads broken, proving that Paul had been
around the house.

00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:35.860
She also checks upstairs on her dresser, which
would have been impossible for Paul to reach

00:30:35.860 --> 00:30:40.070
with his broken legs, and finds that her hairs
have supposedly been completely removed.

00:30:40.070 --> 00:30:43.280
It could be that they just weren’t set up
perfectly and they fell away on their own,

00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:46.640
but I would guess that it could be related
to her aforementioned memory problems -- she

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:50.560
probably planned to set up the threads on her dresser
but never actually did, but because she knew

00:30:50.560 --> 00:30:52.900
he’d been out of the room, she blames him
for that as well.

00:30:52.900 --> 00:30:56.980
She also realizes the butcher knife in the
kitchen was missing, so while he’s asleep

00:30:56.980 --> 00:31:00.900
she searches around under the bed, where she
finds that, and some pills he’d been storing

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:01.400
there.

00:31:01.460 --> 00:31:04.860
This came as a surprise to her, and she must
have decided that she had seen enough.

00:31:04.860 --> 00:31:09.250
She had to put and end to his rummaging around
before he found a way to get an upper hand.

00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:12.290
To do so she turned to a technique originating
in South Africa.

00:31:12.290 --> 00:31:15.990
As Paul woke up that morning she injected
him with a pre-op shot.

00:31:15.990 --> 00:31:19.720
She reveals that she found the bobby pin he
used to escape the first time, the broken

00:31:19.720 --> 00:31:22.910
threads, and the butcher’s knife, which
she angrily throws into the wall, where it

00:31:22.910 --> 00:31:24.050
sticks like a dart.

00:31:24.050 --> 00:31:28.270
She explains that Paul’s tricks won’t
fly by her, she’s been a nurse for 10 years.

00:31:28.270 --> 00:31:32.120
This is another example of her memory issues
or her losing her mind, because if you were

00:31:32.120 --> 00:31:35.960
paying attention in the beginning of this
video, you may remember she became nurse in

00:31:35.960 --> 00:31:38.480
1966, which was 21 years beforehand.

00:31:38.480 --> 00:31:40.330
She asks how many times he’d left the room.

00:31:40.330 --> 00:31:43.020
He insists it was 3 times, but she believes
it’s more.

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:46.880
“I guess fellows like you must get so used
to lying for a living that you just can’t

00:31:46.880 --> 00:31:48.340
stop doing it in real life.

00:31:48.340 --> 00:31:49.490
But that’s all right, Paul.

00:31:49.490 --> 00:31:53.520
Because the principle doesn’t change if
you were out seven times, or seventy, or seventy

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:54.570
times seven.

00:31:54.570 --> 00:31:57.560
The principle doesn’t change, and neither
does the response.”

00:31:57.560 --> 00:32:01.340
The seventy times seven thing is not another
Brand New reference cause they didn’t exist

00:32:01.340 --> 00:32:05.230
yet, but rather a biblical reference towards
the belief in an eternal forgiveness from

00:32:05.230 --> 00:32:09.660
God, who is supposed to forgive a man’s
sins not seven times, not seventy times seven

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:10.660
times, but always.

00:32:10.660 --> 00:32:13.840
So Annie is basically saying, she doesn’t
care how many times Paul has been out, she’s

00:32:13.840 --> 00:32:16.320
still gonna punish him with a process called
hobbling.

00:32:17.840 --> 00:32:20.940
"Paul, do you know about the early days at the Kimberly Diamond Mines?

00:32:21.860 --> 00:32:24.440
Do you know what they did to the native workers who stole diamonds?

00:32:24.940 --> 00:32:27.680
If they caught them, they had to make sure they could go on working.

00:32:28.040 --> 00:32:30.520
But they also had to make sure they could never run away.

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:32.860
The operation was called hobbling."

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:36.660
She plans to dismember his foot using very
makeshift tools.

00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:40.250
She has a bottle of Betadine to disinfect
the wound, which she slathers over his leg,

00:32:40.250 --> 00:32:41.690
then brings out the axe.

00:32:41.690 --> 00:32:45.550
She also has a freaking propane torch (!) That
didn’t make the movie.

00:32:45.550 --> 00:32:49.790
She chops the axe down into his ankle, causing
excruciating pain, but Annie, after taking

00:32:49.790 --> 00:32:54.430
out over 40 people in her lifetime showed
no sympathy and she reared back again to cut

00:32:54.430 --> 00:32:55.800
clean through his ankle.

00:32:55.800 --> 00:32:59.440
That’s where the blow torch comes in -- she
uses that to burn his stump to a crisp, thus

00:32:59.440 --> 00:33:01.120
cauterizing the wound.

00:33:01.120 --> 00:33:04.430
This causes the bedsheet to catch fire, and
she’s prepared with a bucket of water to

00:33:04.430 --> 00:33:05.820
dump over everything.

00:33:05.820 --> 00:33:10.200
It seems that Annie then started to realize
how dangerous her barbaric torture method was.

00:33:10.220 --> 00:33:13.880
She told him he’d be fine, even as he was
screaming from the worst pain imaginable,

00:33:13.880 --> 00:33:17.210
but she sounded nervous as she did so and
seemed to be looking around the room.

00:33:17.210 --> 00:33:20.870
I think she realized that the procedure had
come way too close to killing him, and was

00:33:20.870 --> 00:33:23.960
worried that she’d never get to read the
end of her book because of it.

00:33:23.960 --> 00:33:27.810
This fear kept up for the next week and he
was in a semi-comatose state, and she wouldn’t

00:33:27.810 --> 00:33:31.121
allow him to go back to work on the novel
when he started to come out of it, because

00:33:31.121 --> 00:33:33.960
she was very nervous that he was on the brink
of death.

00:33:33.960 --> 00:33:37.540
When he did go back to his writing she collected
his work at the end of every day, eager to

00:33:37.550 --> 00:33:38.700
get her fix of fiction.

00:33:38.710 --> 00:33:42.450
If Annie were around today, she would likely
be one of those people who exercise no self

00:33:42.450 --> 00:33:46.220
control when it comes to the binge-release
strategy seen on certain streaming platforms,

00:33:46.220 --> 00:33:49.140
where an entire season of a show will be released
at once.

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:52.020
Earlier I mentioned that when she went into
the manic-episode, it was accompanied by the

00:33:52.020 --> 00:33:56.270
binge-eating of unhealthy snacks, and I briefly
mentioned that it shows her lack of self control

00:33:56.270 --> 00:33:59.640
which is paralleled by her lack of self control
in consuming her favorite media.

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:03.220
So when she comes in to try to bribe Paul
into telling her the entire rest of the story,

00:34:03.220 --> 00:34:06.830
it’s no coincidence that she uses ice cream
sundaes to try to do so.

00:34:06.830 --> 00:34:10.760
Paul wisely chooses not to tell her the ending
though, since the anticipation for the book was

00:34:10.760 --> 00:34:12.620
the only thing keeping him alive.

00:34:12.620 --> 00:34:16.690
As the Spring season was winding down Annie
must have been in a particularly bad mental

00:34:16.690 --> 00:34:20.070
state, and when she heard Paul complaining
about the missing N key on the typewriter,

00:34:20.070 --> 00:34:23.990
she wanted to give him something new to worry
about, and came back with the disinfectant,

00:34:23.990 --> 00:34:28.180
a syringe and an electric knife, ready to
perform another “operation”.

00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:41.320
After Paul’s complaint about the typewriter,
Annie stormed into the kitchen in another

00:34:41.320 --> 00:34:42.320
bi-polar episode.

00:34:42.320 --> 00:34:45.870
She was throwing around objects and screaming,
then she came back into the room and jabbed

00:34:45.870 --> 00:34:46.870
the syringe into Paul.

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:50.940
It caused him to become drowsy and defenseless,
and once again she poured on the Betadine

00:34:50.940 --> 00:34:54.320
and held down his hand as he screamed and
pleaded with her.

00:34:54.320 --> 00:34:57.720
After cutting off the thumb with her electric
knife, she took it with her back into the

00:34:57.720 --> 00:35:00.060
kitchen and got to work on baking a cake.

00:35:00.060 --> 00:35:03.370
This was part of her plan to remind Paul who
was really in charge.

00:35:03.370 --> 00:35:06.850
She felt he was getting too comfortable when
he started asking for things again, so this

00:35:06.850 --> 00:35:09.460
was her psychotic way of setting the record
straight.

00:35:09.460 --> 00:35:12.980
She stuck his loose thumb in the birthday
cake like a candle and brought it into the

00:35:12.980 --> 00:35:14.720
room singing Happy Birthday.

00:35:14.720 --> 00:35:18.450
Although it was not actually Paul’s birthday,
he got the idea when she told him: “If you

00:35:18.450 --> 00:35:22.560
promise to be good Paul you can have a piece
of birthday cake but you won’t have to eat

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:24.120
any of the special candle.”

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:28.020
From there, she only gets weirder and crazier,
which may have had something to do with the

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:31.680
fact that Paul was closing in on finishing
the book and she knew their time was coming

00:35:31.680 --> 00:35:33.500
to an end one way or another.

00:35:33.500 --> 00:35:37.190
Over a month later, a car appears at her house
before she has the chance to gag Paul again,

00:35:37.190 --> 00:35:41.180
and she hears shattering glass and immediately
knows he’s trying to get the person’s

00:35:41.180 --> 00:35:41.680
attention.

00:35:41.680 --> 00:35:45.400
She grabs a sharpened cross -- you know, something
that almost anyone would have lying around

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:47.900
the house -- and goes out to discover it’s
a police officer.

00:35:47.900 --> 00:35:51.790
The guy, whose name was Dywane Kushner, was
stunned to have found Paul and didn’t even

00:35:51.790 --> 00:35:56.380
notice Annie before she marched out and stabbed
him in the back and stomach several times,

00:35:56.380 --> 00:35:58.270
sending him crashing to the ground.

00:35:58.270 --> 00:36:01.710
She leaves him barely alive,
and then comes and finishes the job with her

00:36:01.710 --> 00:36:04.840
ridable lawn mower, using it to run over his
arm and head.

00:36:04.840 --> 00:36:07.560
"It's the ninth annual Dallas mower expo!

00:36:07.720 --> 00:36:09.140
Be there -- ere

00:36:09.140 --> 00:36:11.560
As the biggest names in mowers, hedges and clippers -- cutting technology!

00:36:11.660 --> 00:36:14.260
Seaking! Oil painintgs. Seaking!

00:36:14.260 --> 00:36:17.420
Don't miss the Dallas Mower Expo oscare oops ott

00:36:17.440 --> 00:36:20.720
BE THERE! EERRRREEE!!!

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:25.280
Annie spends the afternoon casually cleaning
up the body and erasing any evidence, singing

00:36:25.280 --> 00:36:27.100
“She’ll Be Comin’ Round The Mountain”
as she did so.

00:36:27.100 --> 00:36:31.390
I kind of wish this was in the movie, because
it shows how doing something so vile can be

00:36:31.390 --> 00:36:35.770
a source of happiness for her, and I think
if done right, it would be creepy in the same

00:36:35.770 --> 00:36:39.480
way as Jack reciting the Three Little Pigs
nursery rhyme in The Shining.

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:43.000
She knows that eventually more cops will come
looking for Kushner, so she devises a plan

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:45.350
to give Paul a little more time to finish
the novel.

00:36:45.350 --> 00:36:47.200
She doesn’t care what happens to her after
that.

00:36:47.200 --> 00:36:50.570
She starts by dragging a mattress down to
the cellar and brings Paul down there so that

00:36:50.570 --> 00:36:53.860
he can’t alert the police again if they
come back while she goes to hide the police

00:36:53.860 --> 00:36:55.010
car at her laughing place.

00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:58.560
There’s also an interesting conversation
that takes place with Paul, where we find

00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:03.330
out she actually believes she’s not crazy,
she sees herself as a good samaritan because

00:37:03.330 --> 00:37:04.930
she saved Paul’s life.

00:37:04.930 --> 00:37:05.930
Annie said.

00:37:05.930 --> 00:37:07.360
“I was raised better even if you weren’t.

00:37:07.360 --> 00:37:09.630
You’re lucky I didn’t cut off your man-gland.

00:37:09.630 --> 00:37:10.920
I thought of it, you know.”

00:37:10.920 --> 00:37:11.800
YIKES!

00:37:11.800 --> 00:37:14.620
She then goes on to explain her entire plan
to him.

00:37:14.620 --> 00:37:18.010
She’s gonna hide the car in a shed, bury
the cop and ride her ex-husbands trail bike

00:37:18.010 --> 00:37:19.010
to get back.

00:37:19.010 --> 00:37:22.390
She’ll leave a note on the door saying she
went off to a ceramics show in Steamboat Heaven,

00:37:22.390 --> 00:37:26.160
but if they ask her about it later she’ll
say she slept in her car rather than a hotel

00:37:26.160 --> 00:37:28.160
so that nobody asks her to produce receipts.

00:37:28.160 --> 00:37:31.710
She also says that she’ll be keeping the
deceased officer’s gun in her purse, so

00:37:31.710 --> 00:37:35.800
if Paul tries to scream whenever the cops
do show up, she could reasonably take them

00:37:35.800 --> 00:37:37.230
out before they’re able to react.

00:37:37.230 --> 00:37:41.211
She does all that the next morning, and the
day after, the cops do show up as expected.

00:37:41.211 --> 00:37:44.880
She explains that the first officer came around
looking for Paul right as she was about to

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:48.920
leave, and she told him she did recognize
the man in the photo because it was her favorite

00:37:48.920 --> 00:37:49.420
writer.

00:37:49.420 --> 00:37:53.080
She also explains that she supposedly offered
the guy a cold Pepsi on his way out, and that

00:37:53.090 --> 00:37:56.210
he had taken it with him -- and Annie had
planted a Pepsi bottle with his fingerprints

00:37:56.210 --> 00:37:59.540
on it a couple miles up the road to make it
look like he had passed her house safely.

00:37:59.540 --> 00:38:03.130
She’s able to send these new cops on their
way without incident, but it wasn’t long

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:06.960
before news broke that the Dragon Lady was
under investigation once again.

00:38:06.960 --> 00:38:10.860
Three days later a news crew shows up, which
especially got under Annie’s skin because

00:38:10.860 --> 00:38:14.630
the media was probably her least favorite
part when she was on trial in Denver for her

00:38:14.630 --> 00:38:16.480
actions as the Head Maternity Nurse.

00:38:16.480 --> 00:38:19.880
She fires a warning shot with her gun to scare
them away, but when she comes back in, her

00:38:19.880 --> 00:38:24.460
old paranoia about the town being out to get
her returns and sends her into another manic-depressive

00:38:24.460 --> 00:38:27.960
episode -- scratching and slapping herself
to the point of drawing blood.

00:38:27.960 --> 00:38:31.500
More police show up the next day, and they
inquire about the injuries she’d given herself,

00:38:31.500 --> 00:38:34.040
so she tells them she scratched herself during
a bad dream.

00:38:34.040 --> 00:38:37.741
Her secret was unraveling, and she predicted
that they’d be back before long with a search

00:38:37.741 --> 00:38:38.440
warrant.

00:38:38.440 --> 00:38:42.120
The next day gawkers from in town came by
to look at the house, which continued to unnerve

00:38:42.120 --> 00:38:42.620
her.

00:38:42.620 --> 00:38:46.160
At one point a car full of teenagers comes
onto the driveway to make fun of the Dragon

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:48.960
Lady, and she furiously threatens to shoot
them if they don’t leave.

00:38:48.960 --> 00:38:53.310
If only it was the late 2000s, she would have
known all you have to do is tuck in your clothes

00:38:53.310 --> 00:38:55.100
and strike a violent pose.

00:38:55.100 --> 00:38:58.420
Considering that even just being bothered
by her neighbors, The Roidmans, who lived

00:38:58.430 --> 00:39:02.760
down the road got her in a rage before, this
new wave of attention was really getting to

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:06.381
her, but she’s able to get through it when
Paul delivers the good news: Misery’s Return

00:39:06.381 --> 00:39:08.360
should be done the next evening.

00:39:08.360 --> 00:39:11.990
When the morning of the big day arrived, Annie
excitedly came in to bring him his breakfast

00:39:11.990 --> 00:39:15.630
and pain medication just as she had when he
first started the book, so I’m gonna use

00:39:15.630 --> 00:39:16.980
the exact same footage right here.

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:20.760
Later that day she brings in a caviar dish in celebration of the
book’s completion.

00:39:20.770 --> 00:39:24.160
She had never had caviar before, and when
she tried it, she didn’t like the taste.

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:27.400
I’m not entirely sure how to interpret that
-- it could be King leaving his readers a

00:39:27.400 --> 00:39:31.200
clue about who would win and who would lose,
or it could be something deeper than that.

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:34.540
She informs him that when the last word is
written, his favorite bottle of champagne

00:39:34.540 --> 00:39:38.120
will be waiting for him -- and he requests
to be allowed to practice one other ritual

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:40.710
he has for finishing a book: smoking his cigarette.

00:39:40.710 --> 00:39:44.710
Although she finds smoking to be disgusting,
she allows it as long as she’s out of the

00:39:44.710 --> 00:39:45.710
room as he does it.

00:39:45.710 --> 00:39:49.480
She’s probably in a charitable mood out
of excitement to finally find out of the story

00:39:49.480 --> 00:39:50.000
ends.

00:39:50.000 --> 00:39:53.380
She brings the smoke and the match into the
room around noon and leaves him to complete

00:39:53.380 --> 00:39:55.480
the book until he calls her in to read the
final chapters.

00:39:55.480 --> 00:39:59.700
But as soon as she gets into the room, she
knows something is wrong when she smells lighter

00:39:59.700 --> 00:40:00.420
fluid.

00:40:00.420 --> 00:40:04.560
She freezes and see Paul holding the lit match
over a soaked stack of papers.

00:40:04.560 --> 00:40:08.070
He sets it on fire, and that hurts her more
than anything he could have possibly done

00:40:08.070 --> 00:40:11.880
to her, because reading this book was the
only thing she left she had left to live for.

00:40:11.880 --> 00:40:15.860
She screamed and rushed to try to put out
the flames, in her delusional state she still

00:40:15.860 --> 00:40:17.500
felt some of it could be salvaged.

00:40:17.500 --> 00:40:21.560
She keeps saying “Not Misery, Not Misery”
another sign that she was living more in the

00:40:21.560 --> 00:40:24.950
fictional world than the real one -- she had
never shown this kind of compassion out care

00:40:24.950 --> 00:40:26.440
for any of her victims.

00:40:26.440 --> 00:40:30.160
As she’s trying to save the burning pages,
she doesn’t notice Paul lift up the typewriter

00:40:30.160 --> 00:40:32.490
and smash it down upon the center of her back.

00:40:32.490 --> 00:40:36.550
This knocks her down into the flames, and
she screams even louder as her skin burns.

00:40:36.550 --> 00:40:39.770
She hollers at Paul that she’s going to
kill him, but she trips over the typewriter

00:40:39.770 --> 00:40:43.830
-- ironically the thing that she cheaped out
on and caused so many issues for Paul, including

00:40:43.830 --> 00:40:46.780
the loss of his thumb, was finally coming
back to bite Annie.

00:40:46.780 --> 00:40:50.320
He falls on top of her and she struggles to
claw her way out under his weight.

00:40:50.320 --> 00:40:54.280
Then he begins to stuff the burning piles
of ash into her mouth, choking her just as

00:40:54.280 --> 00:40:56.350
she had nearly choked him with the dust rag.

00:40:56.350 --> 00:40:59.830
Eventually she’s able to buck him off of
her, but she ends up tripping on the typewriter

00:40:59.830 --> 00:41:00.830
a second time.

00:41:00.830 --> 00:41:03.060
I’ve heard of tripping on your words but
this is ridiculous.

00:41:03.060 --> 00:41:07.290
This time her head slams against the mantelpiece
and she goes down, knocking her out for some

00:41:07.290 --> 00:41:08.290
time.

00:41:08.290 --> 00:41:11.810
She comes to and sees Paul trying to get away,
but again, her previous actions come back

00:41:11.810 --> 00:41:12.810
to bite her.

00:41:12.810 --> 00:41:16.640
She’s unable to hold onto his leg with no
foot to grab onto and he gets away and closes

00:41:16.640 --> 00:41:17.790
the door behind him.

00:41:17.790 --> 00:41:21.980
But desperate to have her revenge on the man
who killed her Misery not once, but twice,

00:41:21.980 --> 00:41:25.650
she makes her way to the door and reaches
her fingers underneath to try to grab his

00:41:25.650 --> 00:41:28.850
clothes, but is unsuccessful at holding onto
him and passes out again.

00:41:28.850 --> 00:41:32.150
When she wakes up she’s able to remove the
burnt paper from her throat and she has some

00:41:32.150 --> 00:41:36.310
time to get ahold of herself and make one
last go at trying to bring Paul down with

00:41:36.310 --> 00:41:36.800
her.

00:41:36.800 --> 00:41:40.340
She’s pretty sure he’s still in the house
because her car is still there and as far

00:41:40.350 --> 00:41:42.500
as she could tell nobody had come to rescue
him.

00:41:42.500 --> 00:41:47.380
The doors were also all locked making it very
unlikely that he’d get out on his own.

00:41:47.380 --> 00:41:51.100
She jumps out the guest room window and makes
her way to the barn, where she would pick

00:41:51.110 --> 00:41:53.080
out a tool to exact her revenge.

00:41:53.080 --> 00:41:58.560
She grabs her chainsaw and with it in her
hand, while making her way out the barn, she

00:41:58.560 --> 00:42:00.330
collapsed for the final time.

00:42:00.330 --> 00:42:03.780
Police later discovered her and determined
that she had succumbed to her fractured skull.

00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:06.070
It was the typewriter that had really done
her in.

00:42:06.070 --> 00:42:10.920
In the end, Annie would be known by the community
as far more than a stalker fan of a romance

00:42:10.920 --> 00:42:11.600
novelist.

00:42:11.600 --> 00:42:15.700
The community would eventually discover her
scrapbook and trail of more than 40 crimes.

00:42:15.700 --> 00:42:19.760
But to Paul Sheldon, who had dealt with letters
from many obsessed fans in the past, running

00:42:19.760 --> 00:42:23.850
into the the craziest of them was his worst
nightmare and for the world famous author

00:42:23.850 --> 00:42:28.380
Stephen King, Annie was a combination of every
uncomfortable fan experience wrapped up and

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:31.010
taken to the worst possible scenario.

00:42:31.010 --> 00:42:34.680
There are many more characters you have to
understand to have a full grasp of the Stephen

00:42:34.680 --> 00:42:38.400
King Universe, so click that playlist on the
left to hear about the histories of his other

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:39.400
characters.

00:42:39.400 --> 00:42:43.850
And if you love horror, remember to subscribe
to CZsWorld for new horrors every week ring

00:42:43.850 --> 00:42:48.980
the deathbell for notifications and I’ll
see you in the next one, assuming we stay

00:42:48.980 --> 00:42:50.620
inside.

